  "image_name": "2b18f12303940b9d61585238818ba8ca.png",
  "prompt_text": "The figure summarizes a proposed vicious cycle linking adipose tissue dysfunction, insulin resistance, and androgen excess in polycystic ovary syndrome (PCOS). It starts with normal adipose tissue becoming dysfunctional, marked by enlarged adipocytes, impaired formation of new fat cells, and dysregulated lipolysis. The abnormal fat tissue also shows macrophage infiltration, indicating inflammation.\n\nThis dysfunctional adipose tissue then releases more free fatty acids, pro-inflammatory cytokines such as IL-6 and TNF, and altered adipokines. These signals affect major metabolic organs including the liver, pancreas, and skeletal muscle, leading to ectopic lipid accumulation, lipotoxicity, low-grade inflammation, and oxidative stress. Together these changes promote insulin resistance.\n\nInsulin resistance is shown feeding back to the ovary and adrenal gland, contributing to excess androgen production. Increased AKR1C3 expression appears to enhance conversion of androstenedione to testosterone, further increasing androgen levels. The resulting androgen excess then worsens adipose tissue abnormalities, pushing the tissue farther from its normal state.\n\nOverall, the diagram depicts PCOS as a self-reinforcing loop in which dysfunctional fat tissue, systemic metabolic stress, and hyperandrogenism amplify one another.",
